Q: How do I define a type variable for my generic class to be type of a class or subclass? A: <pre><code>from enum import Enum
from typing import Generic, TypeVar


class ItemSize(Enum):
    BIG = 1
    MEDIUM = 2
    SMALL = 3


class Item:
    def __init__(self, name: str, size: ItemSize):
        self.name = name
        self.size = size


class BigItem(Item):
    def __init__(self, name: str):
        super().__init__(name, ItemSize.BIG)


T = TypeVar(&quot;T&quot;, bound=Item)


class ItemRegistry(Generic[T], dict[str, T]):
    def add_item(self, item: T) -&gt; None:
        self[item.name] = item


class BigItemRegistry(ItemRegistry[BigItem]):
    pass
</code></pre>

Q: How to work with string fields in a C struct? A: <p><strong>On strings and memory allocation:</strong></p>
<p>A string in C is just a sequence of <code>char</code>s, so you can use <code>char *</code> or a <code>char</code> array wherever you want to use a string data type:</p>
<pre><code>typedef struct     {
  int number;
  char *name;
  char *address;
  char *birthdate;
  char gender;
} patient;
</code></pre>
<p>Then you need to allocate memory for the structure itself, and for each of the strings:</p>
<pre><code>patient *createPatient(int number, char *name, 
  char *addr, char *bd, char sex) {

  // Allocate memory for the pointers themselves and other elements
  // in the struct.
  patient *p = malloc(sizeof(struct patient));

  p-&gt;number = number; // Scalars (int, char, etc) can simply be copied

  // Must allocate memory for contents of pointers.  Here, strdup()
  // creates a new copy of name.  Another option:
  // p-&gt;name = malloc(strlen(name)+1);
  // strcpy(p-&gt;name, name);
  p-&gt;name = strdup(name);
  p-&gt;address = strdup(addr);
  p-&gt;birthdate = strdup(bd);
  p-&gt;gender = sex;
  return p;
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you'll only need a few <code>patient</code>s, you can avoid the memory management at the expense of allocating more memory than you really need:</p>
<pre><code>typedef struct     {
  int number;
  char name[50];       // Declaring an array will allocate the specified
  char address[200];   // amount of memory when the struct is created,
  char birthdate[50];  // but pre-determines the max length and may
  char gender;         // allocate more than you need.
} patient;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>On linked lists:</strong></p>
<p>In general, the purpose of a linked list is to provide quick access to an ordered collection of elements.  If your <code>llist</code> contains an element called <code>num</code> (which presumably contains the patient number), you need an additional data structure to hold the actual <code>patient</code>s themselves, and you'll need to look up the patient number every time.</p>
<p>Instead, if you declare</p>
<pre><code>typedef struct llist
{
  patient *p;
  struct llist *next;
} list;
</code></pre>
<p>then each element contains a direct pointer to a <code>patient</code> structure, and you can access the data like this:</p>
<pre><code>patient *getPatient(list *patients, int num) {
  list *l = patients;
  while (l != NULL) {
    if (l-&gt;p-&gt;num == num) {
      return l-&gt;p;
    }
    l = l-&gt;next;
  }
  return NULL;
}
</code></pre>

Q: How should I prevent duplicate processing of events in a distributed system? A: <p>Design for at-least-once and make processing idempotent. That's the answer to your last question, and it determines the rest.</p>
<p>Kafka does have exactly-once semantics, but that only holds when the sink is Kafka. Your sink is a payment, so the guarantee stops at the broker boundary and you still need idempotent processing. Also worth noting you don't say which broker you're on, and most of them (SQS, RabbitMQ, Pub/Sub) offer nothing like this.</p>
<p>Also, put dedup in the database that owns the business state, in the same transaction as the effect:</p>
<pre><code>begin;
  insert into processed_events (event_id) values ('evt_12345')
  on conflict do nothing;
  -- 0 rows affected means already handled, roll back and ack
  update accounts set balance = balance + 1000 where user_id = 'user_42';
commit;
</code></pre>
<p>you can read more about producer delivery here: <a href="https://docs.confluent.io/kafka/design/delivery-semantics.html#producer-delivery" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://docs.confluent.io/kafka/design/delivery-semantics.html#producer-delivery</a></p>

Q: SDL window not moving, what to do? A: <p>Not an Ubuntu thing, your program needs to pumps the event queue.</p>
<p>SDL only does that work when you call into it, via <code>SDL_PollEvent()</code>, <code>SDL_WaitEvent()</code> or <code>SDL_PumpEvents()</code>. So swap the delays for a real loop, something like below:</p>
<pre><code>void game_run(Game *game) {
  bool running = true;
  while (running) {
    SDL_Event event;
    while (SDL_PollEvent(&amp;event)) {
      if (event.type == SDL_EVENT_QUIT) running = false;
    }

    SDL_SetRenderDrawColor(game-&gt;renderer, 255, 255, 255, 255);
    SDL_RenderClear(game-&gt;renderer);
    SDL_RenderPresent(game-&gt;renderer);
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you really did want it to show for a few seconds and then quit, keep the loop and add a deadline with <code>SDL_GetTicks()</code> instead of sleeping.</p>
